  "slug": "design-topic-31e9",
  "summary": "Four recent preprints apply metabolomics, in silico, AI-guided, and HDX-MS approaches to resolve protein interactions in complex systems.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints, the dominant thread is the mapping of molecular interactions under conditions that extend beyond purified components. A metabolomics workflow combining HPLC fractionation with STD NMR aims to identify endogenous low-affinity metabolite ligands of fructosamine-3-kinase, while an integrative in silico pipeline with transcriptomics and structural bioinformatics nominates 27 putative human protein targets of plastic-derived chemicals. An AI agent-guided pipeline reports a small molecule that disrupts LAG-3 interactions with tau and amyloid-beta while sparing MHC II and FGL1. Crude-HDX extends hydrogen-deuterium exchange mass spectrometry from purified proteins to lysates and intact cells, applied to KRAS and SOScat. The new element is the shift toward target and ligand discovery in native or crowded cellular contexts. The items are heterogeneous, with computational and experimental approaches differing in scope and maturity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
